        "original_id": "6b8b6623-d6f7-52ad-af49-f28ceea03ed8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jacques Demy, Paul Sloane, Cecil B. DeMille, Stepin Fetchit, Cary Grant, Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott, Walter Brennan\n- Works: The Umbrellas of Cherbourg, The Young Girls of Rochefort, Hearts in Dixie, The Woman Accused, The Texans, Break of Day, Eve's Leaves\n- Topics: film directing, film writing, film adaptation, film production, film distribution\n\nFacts:\n- Paul Sloane was born on April 19, 1893, and died on November 15, 1963.\n- Paul Sloane directed 26 films from 1925 to 1952 and wrote or co-wrote 35 films.\n- Hearts in Dixie was released in 1929 and featured Stepin Fetchit.\n- The Woman Accused was released in 1933 and featured Cary Grant.\n- The Texans featured Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott, and Walter Brennan.\n- Jacques Demy is best known for directing the mid-1960s musicals The Umbrellas of Cherbourg (1964) and The Young Girls of Rochefort (1967).\n- Eve's Leaves was directed by Paul Sloane and was produced and distributed by Cecil B. DeMille.\n- Break of Day was adapted into a 1980 film directed by Jacques Demy.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "dc67e859-d064-58fa-a03d-9bd08c482a80",
        "text": "Page: Jacques Demy. Sentence: He is best known for the two musicals he directed in the mid-1960s: \"The Umbrellas of Cherbourg\" (1964) and \"The Young Girls of Rochefort\" (1967).\nPage: Paul Sloane (director). Sentence: Paul Sloane (April 19, 1893 – November 15, 1963) was a writer and film director who directed 26 films from 1925 to 1952, and wrote or co-wrote 35 films.\nPage: Paul Sloane (director). Sentence: His movies include \"Hearts in Dixie\" (1929) with Stepin Fetchit, \"The Woman Accused\" (1933) with Cary Grant, and \"The Texans\" with Joan Bennett, Randolph Scott and Walter Brennan.\nPage: Break of Day. Sentence: It was adapted into a 1980 film directed by Jacques Demy.\nPage: Eve's Leaves. Sentence: The film was produced and distributed by Cecil B. DeMille and directed by Paul Sloane.",
